vings  rates to attract deposits they would  lend that money out at a higher rate for  things like mortgages and credit cards  but that's changed in recent years as  more money has flowed into banks and  those banks have found fewer attractive  options for making loans since congress  passed the cares act in 2020 americans  have failed increased savings and many  are stashing it in banks even though  they're earning next to nothing in part  because the federal reserve has held u.s  interest rates close to zero for much of  the past decade folks have just gotten  used to not getting anything for their  savings the fed has also flooded the  market with cash on the bank side  they're looking to make money and very  few loans can offer the kind of return  that banks can earn from investing their  customers deposits in stocks bonds and  other assets plus there's been a decline  in demand for loans as many small  businesses were wiped out by the  coronavirus pandemic  this is basic supply and demand  right now there's a ton of money being  supplied to banks without a  corresponding increase in demand  that lowers value even for something  like money the result of all that is  big banks are getting americans money  for next to nothing and they're able to  earn high rates of return with  historically low risk  you can see why they're earning record  profits seemingly every quarter savvy  investors are pulling their money out of  traditional savings accounts the real  savings rate which is the difference  between an average interest rate on a us  savings account and the rate of  inflation was the most negative it has  ever been in february negative 7.8  that environment is pushing many  americans to take more risk some are  taking out loans and using that money as  a substitute for their savings for  everyday retail investors that can be a  personal or home equity loan from a bank  or a margin loan from their investment  account  they're putting their money in stocks  and other investments and keeping that  borrowed money at the ready in case of  emergencies  professional investors are taking out  margin loans as well and buying treasury  futures or using options contracts to  substitute for cash  investors who can afford it are also  pumping their money into real estate  buying homes and commercial properties  this has been a major contributor to the  extreme run up in housing prices make no  mistake these are risky strategies but  many see that risk as a better option  than having their money eroded by  inflation at the fastest rate in history  it remains to be seen just how high  interest rates can go without banks  giving their customers a bump in their  savings rate but until americans start  demanding higher rates  banks aren't going to pay them if you  enjoyed this video hit the like button  and make sure to subscribe if you're  interested in markets and want to see  more videos like this one as ohio  hip-hop legend stally tells us no  surprise in your eyes when you see us  touch the sky with no leer jets if money  call we pop right up like a fair catch  see you next time  [Music]  you

